A||N||20||Lewis||Diagnostic Software||||5||
Z||000000||Lewis||03-16-2000||11:56 AM||||Does anyone use the diagnostic software that could be installed on laptop to read the ECM problems and other computer related items.  You use to be able to purchase this software for  the Fiero at one time...let me know if it is availabe or anyone that has used it..||||reg||5
Z||000001||mshill||03-16-2000||12:17 PM||||This webpage has the software you are looking for.  You will also need an adapter that they describe how to make, but I don't know if they produce them as well.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/software.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/software.htm</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||mwbackus||03-16-2000||12:37 PM||||You can get an Autoxray GM scanner at Checker Auto for about $150.  Unless you are interested in the novelty of having the PC capability, its really not worth the trouble.  I have used my scanner in "real time" monitor mode while driving around to diagnose performance problems on my 88 formula (the idle problems that can occur with certain ECM's in this mode were not apparent).  I consider it an invaluble part of my tool collection.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||03-16-2000||06:11 PM||||Diacom is more expensive but has the advantage of displaying tons of parameters at one time and outputing to files importable to Excel, Lotus, etc.<P>About $250 for Diacom and somewhat more for Diacom+. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||03-17-2000||12:43 AM||||The site you refered to above is run by Frans Godschalk. I bought the components and built the interface thinking this would be very cool to have seeing as I own a laptop.<P>I was dissapointed to find out that the serial data format he developed the interface for is no longer used on my '88 Coupe. (Check to see if there is a terminal in the "E" slot of your ALDL connector). I think it is good for '84-'86.<P>Also, being that he is in the Netherlands and is not a commercial website, it was going to present some difficulty paying him the $15.00 US that he was asking for his software.<P>I have looked extensivly over the web for this same thing to no avial, other than the Diacom software Terryk speaks of. I'm planning on getting the Autoxray unit I think. Probably more robust compared to dragging a $2,500.00 laptop around the shop.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||03-17-2000||02:50 PM||||I was thinking that if someone had the dump format for the serial data coming out of the ALDL, a small embedded uProcessor and an inexpensive LCD readout could be programmed to display the data real time.  I am sure that this is what the scanners do.  I am certain it could be done for much much less than the $150 that they want for the Auto-Xray scanners.  8051 uProcessors are real cheap, so the cost is probably mostly in the LCD display.<P>Is there somehwere on the net that the dump data is defined?||||reg||3
Z||000006||terryk||03-17-2000||03:26 PM||||The biggest issue is that the stream, unlike the later P4 ECM's is not RS-232. It is 9 bit. <P>I did it using a data line on the parallel port and faking a sampler. Then I aquirred a scanner.<P>Either way, the ALDL list for the ECM is available if someone wants to do it. There is software out there that will run on a PC to receive the stream.<P>Try this site:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.techedge.com.au/vehicle/aldl/160/aldl_sw.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.techedge.com.au/vehicle/aldl/160/aldl_sw.htm</A> <P>If someone fires it up, I will give them the stream definitions for the four ECM's (6156, 7748, 6869, 7170). I think those are 'dem!<P>ps,<P>You need to search for a C3 communication program (160 baud). An 8192 baud for a P4/P6 will not work. <P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-17-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ozzy||03-18-2000||02:07 AM||||Is the 8192 Baud data type used on the ECM on my '88 coupe??  <P>Terryk, I checked out the URL you listed. Ironically, it is used for Holden's as far as I can see. Holden is the Australian GM car. <P>ozzy (Aussie!!)||||reg||5
Z||000009||terryk||03-18-2000||10:20 AM||||Yes, that dawned on me after I posted although most of the info applies. Holden is GM of Aus. I was just showing one of several sites that have ALDL communucation software available.<P>All of the Fiero's are C3 computers at 160 baud. None are P4's at 8192 baud. That's one reason I switched to the 7749.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ozzy||03-19-2000||02:06 AM||||O.K. So, I don't really follow your logic here terryk.(I'm a little thick!!) You are obviously very versed in microprocessors and Fiero's. <P>Can I get data from my ECM on the '88 Coupe to my laptop with one of these simple software programs? As I mentioned earlier in this thread, I built the interface designed by Frans shown at the above URL. I only realised after I put it all together that the pin in the ALDL connector ("E" I think) does not even reside in my ALDL conn. There is the 'M' pin however. This is the 8192 baud pin right? What ECM do I have? What is 8192 baud? What did you mean by "faked a sampler"? I have a basic understanding of serial and parallel data streams only. <P>Sorry 2 bug you with all this. I'm just curious if it is possible after all to review the operating parameters of my engine via my laptop. <P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||03-19-2000||07:34 PM||||Hi Ozzy,<P>I looked at the info and the 88 LR8 4 banger is a P4! Cool! Too bad the V6's are still C3. That's why I switched to the 7749 ECM. It's hard to tune when the data updates every 1.25 seconds.<P>The P4's are standard serial 8 bit, 1 start, 1 stop. The levels need to be shifted from 0-5V to the 12V for the RS-232 port. Most people use MAX232/233's since many P4's require that you tell them to shut up and spew the ALDL stream (and therefore you need to talk in both directions on one line), but there are simple transistor circuits for this too.<P>On the pins, if ALDL-D is used, the ECM talks on the Check Engine line (which is why they flicker) at 160 baud. <P>The 160 baud is a strange format of 9-1's followed by 9 bits of data (where the 9th bit is always 1. You have to watch for the high to low transitions, wait and sample the line. I took a printer data line and wrote code to sample the line at 3x the bit rate, find the H-L transitions, wait and grab the bit, then put them all together to make the data bytes. A standard Uart wouldn't handle it that I could find.<P>ALDL-E is 160 baud, but 0-5V. ALDL-M is 8192 baud at 0-5V. The 8192 is easy to work with.<P>There are a few cars that required you to tell them (over the 160 baud serial line) to talk out the 8192 line. Also, some have M and E swapped (some Camaro's and Vette's).<P>In your case, I think it is straight forward dump on Pin M at 8192.<P>Absolutely, you can monitor the stream from your ECM. A must for any serious tuning.<P>I emailed you privately. Reply when you can.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000012||DJRice||03-20-2000||09:20 AM||||Interesting that this would come up again.  I had started this project and built the little gadget to interface the ALDL to the PC serial port then I killed my laptop when I tried a bios upgrade.  I thought about the small uProcessor (specifically a Microchip PIC) and LCD, but wanted to be able to pull it off with software on a PC first.  <P>I just got my laptop back up and running last week (with the exception of the sound &lt;arrgh&gt; ) and had intended to get back on this.||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroSTETZ||03-21-2000||02:13 AM||||hey, what kinda problems are you having with the laptop? i might be able to help if ya need it ( I am a certified technician at CompUSA) same goes for anyone else! any computer related questions, you can email me and i will see if i can help. if i can't, i can at least get you in contact w/ someone who can. Long live the Fiero!||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ozzy||03-21-2000||04:11 PM||||Hey Terry...<P>I have your e-mail and responded. I am on the road and have a weird schedule @ the moment but check my e-mail and this forum daily. Thanx for taking the time to get in touch with me.<P>If you have had the time, let me know about that software & what u think...I will not post the URL here until then. Don't want to get folks up the wrong path.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||03-21-2000||04:22 PM||||Hi Ozzy,<P>I downloaded it at home. I'll play with it tonight and let you know.||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||03-21-2000||07:06 PM||||certified tech at CompUSA? Isn't that an oxymoron? I'm sure you are OK Stetz, after all you do own a Fiero, but around here I wouldn't send people I hate to CompUSA.<P>Reading the ECM stream in real time does require a bit more than the processor and a display. The string comes allong faster than you could visually read it and contains more than 20-50 parameters depending on the ECM in test. You would have to buffer it and focus the display on one variable at a time to be usefull on a small LCD. A laptop has enough screen realestate to display the whole thing at once, but not a 2 line LCD.<P>No, the hardware isn't very expencive. It's the programming involved. The GM only AutoXray is programmed for all the ECM's GM used from 82-95. There is enough diferance that such programming is a pretty large task.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||03-23-2000||11:33 AM||||Thanks for the offer Stetz, but I am need of a new main board to solve my problems.  This all started with a BIOS upgrade gone bad.  The update program erased the Flash ROM of a microcontroller that controls the system configuration, but it did not reprogram it.  It wasnt a removeable chip so that mainboard is pretty well toast.<P>I found a junk laptop on ebay with a busted screen, but it turns out that it had already been parted out because someone spilled a drink in it.  I was able to make everything work on that mainboard except the sound hardware.  <P>The laptop works as long as long as I dont let windows install any sound drivers.  As soon as Windows trys to allocate resources for the soundcard, the system hangs.<P>I bet I never update BIOS on a laptop again.  So if you have access to a HP Omnibook 5500 with a good main board, LMK.<P>(Sorry for the long-winded off-topic explanation to those involved in the original discussion)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||mshill||03-23-2000||01:00 PM||||Ogre, you are right about the LCD, but the idea would be to be able to select what you want to look at with a pushbutton switch that would cycle through the parameters.  You would obviously only be able to see one or two  parameters at a time, but could essentially monitor in real time.<p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 03-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||mwbackus||03-23-2000||01:24 PM||||I would not want to drive around with my ECM in data mode all the time.  A quick scan to see if everything is operating within perimeters is great, but performance is adversly affected during real time data mode.<BR>Maybe a switch to turn on/off would be advisable.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||03-23-2000||02:02 PM||||The C3 ECM's spew data all of the time in normal mode. No, I wouldn't want to lock it in ALDL or backup mode.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Ozzy||04-08-2000||12:03 PM||||FOund another site for those interested that has another ALDL interface and free sofware.<P>The dude will build the interface for you for $60.00 and the software is free. However, the '87,'88 L4 misses out again...<P>All V-6's and 84-86 L4's...<P>There is also some other neat electronic gadgetry here...Bit pricey though!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <A HREF="http://business.fortunecity.com/takeover/175/" TARGET=_blank>http://business.fortunecity.com/takeover/175/</A> <P>Ozzy||||reg||1
